<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Apple</h1></b><br>
    
    Apples are one of the most widely grown fruits in the UK, thriving particularly well in 
    <strong>southern and central regions</strong> due to the milder climate. While they can be cultivated 
    across the country, areas with <strong>warmer summers and lower rainfall</strong>, such as the 
    <strong>South of England</strong>, tend to produce the best-quality apples. In 
    <strong>colder or wetter regions</strong> (Scotland and parts of Northern England), apples can still 
    be grown but may require <strong>sheltered positions</strong> or <strong>greenhouses</strong> for optimal results.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Apples prefer temperate climates with cool winters and mild summers.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> Well-drained, loamy soil with a pH between 6.0 and 7.0 is ideal.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Full sun exposure (at least 6–8 hours per day) is essential.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Dwarf varieties can be grown in pots, while standard trees need more space.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Apple Diseases in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Apple Scab:</strong> Dark, scabby lesions on leaves and fruit. Prevent with resistant varieties and fungicides.<br>
    - <strong>Black Rot:</strong> Brown, sunken spots on apples. Remove infected fruit and improve air circulation.<br>
    - <strong>Cedar Apple Rust:</strong> Bright orange spots on leaves. Avoid planting near junipers and use fungicides.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Apples in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose the right variety:</strong> ‘Cox’s Orange Pippin’, ‘Egremont Russet’, and ‘Braeburn’ grow well in the UK.<br>
    - <strong>Pollination:</strong> Apples need cross-pollination, so plant two or more compatible varieties.<br>
    - <strong>Frost Protection:</strong> In northern areas, protect blossoms from late frosts using fleece.<br>
    - <strong>Pest Control:</strong> Watch for aphids and codling moths. Encourage natural predators like ladybirds.<br><br>

    By following these guidelines, you can successfully grow apples in the UK, whether in a <strong>garden, orchard, or greenhouse</strong>.
    </i></p>
